 A Mac app shows its own settings window before the game, drawn from its `launcher.json` and keeping its choices in the same store ([Saved choices](#saved-choices), [The Mac settings window](settings.md#the-mac-settings-window)). It shows a variable the launch environment or an `env.txt` sets as fixed. A front end that draws the settings itself starts the app with `DM_LAUNCHER=0` in its environment (or `--no-launcher` on its command line) to skip the window. The saved choices still apply, so a front end that writes them gets the same game as the window would. With `DM_PREFLIGHT` set in its environment the app exits at once, before it reads anything, rolls its log or touches the saves: a front end opens a new build that way (through LaunchServices, `NSWorkspace.openApplication`), so macOS runs its first-start check of the bundle then, not when the player starts the game. The check scans every file of an ad hoc signed app and takes minutes with a RomFS of several GB, and macOS remembers it until the app changes.
 
-A Mac game quits when the player holds a controller's Home button for 2 seconds and confirms with A ([On the Mac](playing.md#on-the-mac)). Its prompt is in English; a front end that shows the player another language passes the texts in `DM_HOME_QUIT_TEXT`, one per line in this order: the hold indicator ("Keep holding to quit"), the title ("Quit <game>?"), the note ("Unsaved progress will be lost."), the quit button and the cancel button. An empty or missing line keeps the English text. The same goes for the prompt that waits when a game asks for more players than have joined (Mac and iOS): `DM_CTRL_PROMPT_TEXT`, one per line, the title ("{min} players wanted"), the hint ("Press a button on each controller to join."), the count ("Players: {joined} of {min}"), the done button and the cancel button; `{min}`, `{max}` and `{joined}` are replaced by the numbers. Whether macOS lets the game have the Home button is the player's Home button setting for apps, which a front end reads itself on macOS 27 (`GCControllerHomeButtonSettingsManager`, while a controller is connected) to tell the player when the system keeps Home.
+A Mac game quits when the player holds a controller's Home button for 2 seconds and confirms with A ([On the Mac](playing.md#on-the-mac)). Its prompt is in English; a front end that shows the player another language passes the texts in `DM_HOME_QUIT_TEXT`, one per line in this order: the hold indicator ("Keep holding to quit"), the title ("Quit <game>?"), the note ("Unsaved progress will be lost."), the quit button and the cancel button, then the Home menu's texts: Resume, Back to console mode and its title (the game's name). An empty or missing line keeps the English text, so a front end that passes only the first five still works. The same goes for the prompt that waits when a game asks for more players than have joined (Mac and iOS): `DM_CTRL_PROMPT_TEXT`, one per line, the title ("{min} players wanted"), the hint ("Press a button on each controller to join."), the count ("Players: {joined} of {min}"), the done button and the cancel button; `{min}`, `{max}` and `{joined}` are replaced by the numbers. Whether macOS lets the game have the Home button is the player's Home button setting for apps, which a front end reads itself on macOS 27 (`GCControllerHomeButtonSettingsManager`, while a controller is connected) to tell the player when the system keeps Home.
+
+A short press of Home opens the Home menu over the paused game (`DM_HOME_MENU=0` turns it off): Resume, Quit (which opens the quit prompt) and, when the front end sets `DM_HOME_MENU_LAUNCHER=1`, Back to console mode. That row pauses the game in the background the way a minimized window is paused (input released, focus lost, audio and graphics suspended), leaves full screen, hands the activation to the app named by `DM_LAUNCHER_BUNDLE_ID` (opening it when it is not running) and hides the game. The game resumes when it is active again (the front end activates it, the Dock, ⌘Tab), and on a distributed notification `org.nxnative.resume.<pid>` (`<pid>` is the game's process ID). `org.nxnative.quit.<pid>` quits it as ⌘Q does. It reports its state in the log, `front end: paused in the background` and `front end: resumed`, and as the distributed notifications `org.nxnative.paused.<pid>` and `org.nxnative.resumed.<pid>`. A front end that wants the game in front calls `yieldActivation(toApplicationWithBundleIdentifier:)` or `yieldActivation(to:)` before it activates the game or posts the resume, since macOS lets an app take the activation only from the active one.
 
 Settings a player chose in a front end and wants kept, also for a start from Finder or the Dock, go in the app's saved choices ([Saved choices](#saved-choices)), not in its environment.
 
